Monitoring the maturation progress of machine-picked cotton in Xinjiang after harvest-aid application has long relied on manual field surveys, which are inefficient and difficult to extend to the field scale. Taking defoliation rate and boll opening rate as the two agronomic indicators of cotton maturation status, this dissertation studies UAV-based remote-sensing methods for rapid recognition and their deployment on edge devices. Field imagery involves complex backgrounds and variable illumination, while on-board computing power is limited; accordingly, the dissertation starts with dataset construction, then designs handcrafted-feature and deep recognition models, develops low-light data synthesis and enhancement methods, and validates the workflow through multi-temporal experiments in production fields, so that cotton maturation status can be quantitatively characterized as remote-sensing phenotypic information.

A dual-label dataset of cotton defoliation rate and boll opening rate is constructed first. With a self-developed UAV waypoint-image management tool, MRK positioning records, RGB images, and manual ground-truth surveys from 26 acquisition campaigns between September 24 and October 4, 2024 are linked one by one; after center cropping, rotation and flipping augmentation, and normalization, 8737 image patches of 224×224 pixels are formed. Defoliation rate and boll opening rate are graded into 8 and 6 levels, respectively, and the patches are split into training, validation, and test sets at a 5:1:4 ratio. Experiments show that RGB pixel-vector baselines (PCA + logistic regression and random forest) are noticeably affected by class imbalance, with test Macro-F1 of only 0.30 and 0.40; after switching to a 21-dimensional structured feature set of spectral indices and color statistics combined with LR, RF, and SVM classifiers, the test Macro-F1 of the two indicators rises to 0.909 and 0.851, and the grade MAE drops to within 0.3 levels.

For the recognition model, this dissertation proposes RTCMNet (Real-Time Cotton Monitoring Network), a lightweight dual-indicator joint recognition network that meets the computing constraints of edge deployment. The model adopts a shared backbone, multi-scale convolutional attention (MSCA) blocks, and dual classification heads; with 0.37 M parameters and 0.19 GMACs, it attains a five-fold cross-validation mean Accuracy and Macro-F1 of $0.93 plus.minus 0.02$, close to the $0.94 plus.minus 0.01$ of DenseNet-121 while using only 5.6% of its parameters, and per-image inference latency in edge-side testing drops from 1084 ms to 32 ms. In ablation experiments, the 1,2,2 layer configuration with an 8-head MSCA strikes a good balance between accuracy and efficiency. Since maturation monitoring sometimes has to be carried out under low-light conditions such as sunset, the dissertation further builds a paired low-light data generation pipeline inspired by physical imaging and ISP mechanisms: exposure attenuation, white-balance drift, Poisson-Gaussian noise, mild blur, and vignetting are jointly simulated in the RGB domain, with the statistical distributions calibrated on real nighttime UAV images, providing controllable supervision for training low-light enhancement models.

Building on the low-light data, a lightweight low-light enhancement front-end, $L^3$-AgriUAVNet, is designed and cascaded with RTCMNet into an enhancement-recognition pipeline. Combining HVI color-space transformation with an RFDN-S lightweight backbone, the model has 0.139 M parameters and 6.252 GMACs, achieves the highest SSIM (0.893) on the paired test set with a PSNR of 26.24 dB, and completes per-image inference in 202 ms (4.95 FPS) on an iPad-class tablet. Experiments on real low-light cotton fields show that the dual-indicator prediction distributions before and after enhancement change differently with task type and harvest-aid stage, indicating that cascaded inference is feasible in practice; in a maize-seedling low-light task with detection ground truth, enhanced inputs raise the mAP\@0.5 of YOLOv8n by 14.6% (from 0.1393 to 0.1596). The workflow is validated over multiple dates in two production fields (L1 and L2): recognition results from day 2 to day 21 after harvest-aid application show that both indicator grades rise over time and maintain spatially continuous structures at the field scale; boundary strips with insufficient spray coverage and low-density planting areas caused by low drip-irrigation pressure both exhibit maturation-lagging distributions consistent with field investigations, based on which persistently lagging zones can be located and the efficacy of harvest-aid operations can be assessed.

The dataset, recognition model, and low-light enhancement pipeline built in this dissertation can support intelligent monitoring of cotton maturation and harvest-aid decision-making, and also accumulate usable methods and data for research on agricultural visual perception under low-light conditions.
